  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nNo significant changes to the extended forecast. Upper-level ridging \nwill remain strong across the forecast area Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThe ridge will shift eastward toward the Lower Mississippi Valley \nand Gulf Coast states by the latter part of the week, but our area \nwill still be located along the western influence of this feature. \nHot temperatures will continue through next week, with the highest \nreadings expected Tuesday-Thursday, with high temperatures up to 100-\n105 degrees over southeast New Mexico, the Trans Pecos and portions \nof the Permian Basin. Highs along the Rio Grande may reach up to 105-\n110 degrees. Dry weather conditions will continue Monday and \nTuesday, except for very isolated storms that may develop over the \nDavis Mountains, Marfa Plateau, and/or southern Big Bend. \nAtmospheric moisture may increase over western portions of the \nforecast area during the middle to latter part of the week on the \nwestern side of the ridge. There may be an uptick in convective \ncoverage over our western zones, especially in the higher terrain \nareas Wednesday-Friday. Convective chances will be minimal over the \nPermian Basin and Lower Trans Pecos where subsidence may be \nstronger. Upper-ridging may tend to build westward over our area \nagain by next weekend, so storm chances may be lower over our \nwestern zones. Summer time heat continues into the weekend with \ntemperatures remaining several degrees above normal.",
